// Host → llmd frames on ToolProviderService.Connect, and the reference for
// both protocols that stream carries. A tool host opens Connect to llmd (host =
// gRPC client). The first frame must be executor_hello (preferred) or hello
// (legacy); it selects the protocol for the whole stream, and any other first
// frame is refused with INVALID_ARGUMENT.
//
// 1. Executor protocol (PREFERRED; use it to declare and serve tools from
// another process or Go module). Go hosts should use the public
// github.com/johnewart/pyxis/toolexecutor client rather than speaking it by
// hand.
//
//	host → llmd  ExecutorHello{executor_id, tools[], max_concurrency, version, instance_id}  (first frame)
//	llmd → host  ExecutorHelloAck{accepted_tools, rejected_tools, max_result_bytes}
//	llmd → host  ExecutorToolCall{call_id, tool_name, arguments_json, session_id, tenant_id, origin, max_duration_seconds}
//	llmd → host  ToolCallCancel{call_id, reason}
//	host → llmd  ToolCallResult{call_id, output | failure}  (exactly one per call)
//
// Auth: the tenant comes from llmd-tenant-id request metadata. The shared
// secret configured on llmd (llmdhost.Config.ToolProviderSecret; the
// standalone binary reads LLMD_TOOL_PROVIDER_SHARED_SECRET) is sent in metadata
// key "x-llmd-tool-provider-secret". If llmd has no secret configured, every
// registration is refused (FAILED_PRECONDITION).
//
// Declaring tools: list them in ExecutorHello.tools, each with
// execution = executor{executor_id} equal to the hello's executor_id. llmd
// rejects (with a reason in ExecutorHelloAck.rejected_tools) a tool that has no
// name, is declared twice in the hello, collides with an inline builtin served
// by the llmd host, is already owned by a different executor in the tenant, has
// no executor binding, or whose binding's executor_id differs from the hello.
// An empty tenant or executor_id rejects every tool.
//
// Scope contract: registration is keyed by (tenant, executor_id) and is
// TENANT-WIDE: one stream serves every scope/workspace in the tenant. Each
// ExecutorToolCall carries the calling session's tenant_id, scope_id and
// session_id, so the host binds per-scope state from the call, not the stream.
// scope_id is empty when the call has no resolved scope (and from llmd builds
// that predate the field).
//
// Lifecycle:
//   - Stream ends: pending calls fail as "disconnected, outcome unknown"; the
//     tools stay registered without an owner (still advertised to sessions),
//     and calls to them fail fast with "executor not attached" until an
//     executor with that id says hello again.
//   - A hello with a new instance_id for the same (tenant, executor_id)
//     supersedes the old stream: its pending calls fail as disconnected and it
//     is closed with ABORTED. An empty instance_id is replaced by a random one,
//     so every reconnect then supersedes.
//   - Turn abort: llmd sends ToolCallCancel{reason: "turn_cancelled"} for each
//     in-flight call of the turn and refuses new calls for that turn.
//   - Deadline: each call is bounded by the tool's
//     executor.max_duration_seconds (default 600s), echoed in
//     ExecutorToolCall.max_duration_seconds. On expiry llmd sends
//     ToolCallCancel{reason: "deadline"} ("cancelled" when the caller's context
//     ends first) and stops waiting.
//   - Result size: a ToolCallResult whose serialized size exceeds
//     max_result_bytes (1 MiB) becomes an error result for that call; the
//     stream stays open.
//
// 2. Legacy provider protocol (ToolProviderHello / ToolExecutionInvoke /
// ToolExecutionResult). Registers a stream for the exact (tenant, scope) from
// llmd-tenant-id / llmd-scope-id metadata and declares no tools. llmd still
// uses it as the transport for delegate_to_client tools: the chat loop and the
// MCP server send ToolExecutionInvoke on it when a stream is connected for the
// scope, and the provider replies with ToolExecutionResult keyed by
// execution_id. The secret goes in ToolProviderHello.shared_secret. New
// integrations should use the executor protocol.
